<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP: permitted at the platform level, and the reason is not
  laziness — it is that the platform cannot express the rule this app needs.

  tailnet-byok exists to talk to a service that is already inside a WireGuard
  tunnel. Traffic to 100.64.0.0/10 (the CGNAT range Tailscale allocates) leaves
  the app, is encrypted by the embedded tsnet node, and is decrypted only at the
  destination node. There is no point on the path where a passive observer sees
  the plaintext — including the local Wi-Fi network the phone is attached to.

  That means the honest tradeoff is: requiring HTTPS here would push users
  toward self-signed certificates and "install this CA" flows, which is a
  strictly worse security story than accepting the tunnel as the transport
  boundary. Most self-hosted services on a tailnet (dashboards, DSH-style dev
  servers, Home Assistant, Proxmox) speak plain HTTP behind the tunnel.

  WHY THIS FILE NO LONGER LISTS TAILNET HOSTS (changed 2026-09-13)
  =================================================================
  Android's network security config matches on hostnames. It cannot say
  "100.64.0.0/10", and it cannot know a name that only resolves inside the
  tunnel. The previous revision tried to paper over that with a single
  placeholder entry (`100.64.0.1`), which matches no real node — so a user
  pointing at their own tailnet address got

      communication to 100.101.102.103 not permitted by network security policy

  and could not connect at all. The app's own example target was one of the
  blocked addresses.

  So the rule moved one layer up, to `domain/TailnetAddressPolicy.kt`, which is
  the single gate every destination passes through before it is dialled. It
  knows the real destination, so it can apply the rule the platform could not:
    * cleartext is allowed for tailnet destinations — a 100.x or fd7a:... literal
      (that file checks the ranges as ranges), or a name under *.ts.net, *.local,
      *.internal, *.lan, *.home.arpa, or localhost;
    * cleartext to everything else is REJECTED with
      Reason.CLEARTEXT_REQUIRES_HTTPS, before a socket is opened.

  This file still carries the part the platform must own: the trust anchors, and
  the refusal of user-installed CAs. The address rule is paired with
  `cleartextPermitted()` in that policy object — change one, change the other.

  If you fork this and point it at a public endpoint, keep the pairing: either
  loosen `cleartextPermitted()` deliberately, or make that endpoint HTTPS-only.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <!--
      Cleartext is permitted here because `TailnetAddressPolicy` refuses it for
      every destination outside the tailnet (see above). The platform flag on
      its own would allow cleartext to the public internet; the flag plus that
      check does not.

      System trust anchors only. `src="user"` is deliberately absent. Trusting
      user-installed CAs for every host would mean that anyone who can install a
      root certificate on the device — the user, an MDM profile they did not
      read, or malware with the right permission — can transparently intercept
      HTTPS from this app. That is a real weakening for no benefit here, because
      the case user CAs would serve (a self-signed certificate on your own
      tailnet service) is already covered by the cleartext allowance above: point
      the app at the `100.x` address over http and the tunnel is the transport
      boundary.

      If you fork this and genuinely need a private CA, add it as a pinned
      `<certificates src="@raw/my_ca" />` rather than reopening `user` for
      everything.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

</network-security-config>
